Rules:
- Organize ALL incidents by root cause class. Each `### Root Cause:` is a distinct failure
  mechanism. Use semantic similarity to match (e.g., "targetPort mismatch" and "Service port
  misconfiguration" are the same class).
- If matching existing root cause: increment Seen, append reference, add novel symptoms/detection
  signals, update mitigations if the new session provides a better approach.
- If novel: create a new `### Root Cause:` entry with all subheadings.
- SPLITTING IS DISABLED in this version. Do NOT split an existing entry. If you think a split is
  warranted, keep the original entry intact and note your reasoning in the Causal Chain.
- CONSOLIDATION is allowed: if two or more existing entries describe the same underlying fault
  class, you MAY merge them into a single entry AND declare the merge in the `<merge_result>`
  envelope below (see rules). Never silently delete an entry without declaring it.
- Include causal chains when available. Mark "(validated)" or "(unvalidated)" based on benchmark
  confirmation. Upgrade unvalidated to validated when possible. Skip chains marked "N/A".
- Citations MUST use {% raw %}`{{ref:incidents/FILENAME.md}}`{% endraw %} format exactly.
- MIGRATION: If the prior summary uses the old symptom-first format, restructure into
  root-cause-first format.

Slug rules:
- Every `### Root Cause:` entry MUST contain a `#### Slug: <slug>` line directly under the
  header (before `#### Symptoms`).
- When matching an existing class, PRESERVE its `#### Slug:` line byte-for-byte. A slug is
  sticky: once assigned, it never changes, even if the class name is rewritten.
- When creating a new class, derive the slug from the class name: lowercase, non-alphanumeric
  characters replaced with underscores, truncated to 64 characters. Example:
  "Missing CURRENCY_ADDR env var" → `missing_currency_addr_env_var`.
- When consolidating N entries into one, KEEP the slug of the winning (surviving) entry and
  declare the losers' slugs in the `<merge_result>` envelope.

ABSTRACTION RULES:
- Root causes, causal chains, detection signals, and mitigations must describe the CLASS of
  misconfiguration, not specific instances. Use generic placeholders (<affected-service>,
  <target-port>) instead of specific names or values.
  GOOD: "A Service's targetPort doesn't match the pod's listening port → Connection refused"
  BAD: "text-service targetPort was 9999 while listening on 9090"

Output format:
- Output the updated Long-Term Summary text first, with no preamble.
- Then, on a new line, emit EXACTLY ONE `<merge_result>` block describing the structural
  outcome of this merge. The block must be valid JSON between the tags.

`<merge_result>` contract:

<merge_result>
{
  "primary_action": "new" | "merged" | "noop",
  "primary_class_name": "<class name touched by this run, or null for noop>",
  "reorganizations": [
    {"type": "consolidate", "winner": "<class name A>", "losers": ["<class name B>"]}
  ]
}
</merge_result>

Rules for the envelope:
- `primary_action="new"`: this run introduced a brand new `### Root Cause:` entry.
- `primary_action="merged"`: this run appended to an existing root cause class.
- `primary_action="noop"`: the run produced no material change to the summary.
- `primary_class_name` MUST match the exact text after `### Root Cause:` in the updated summary
  for the class this run touched (or `null` for noop).
- `reorganizations` lists any cross-entry structural changes. Use this only when you are
  consolidating two or more existing entries into one. `winner` is the surviving class name;
  `losers` is the list of absorbed class names. Leave the list empty (`[]`) if no consolidation
  happened.
- Every pre-existing slug listed above must either remain present in the updated summary OR
  appear as a loser in a `consolidate` reorganization. Silently dropping an entry is not allowed.
